Z||000008||BlackGT||02-07-2001||07:37 PM||||I used the lowering kit that the fiero store<BR>sells and as long as you are not driving on pothole riddled pavement it`s fine! The ride is a little bumpy but we ain`t drivin caddies<BR>so it works well for me......this is my daily<BR>driver so I spend a lot of time burning up the roads in and around Atlanta. The kit definately gets rid of that swamp buggy look<BR>and gives it a low mean stance! Sometimes<BR>after I get out of her I walk around and admire her look! Try the kit it works well.<P> ||||reg||6
Z||000009||batboy||02-07-2001||08:32 PM||||If you can afford it, you might consider coil-over kits. They are height adjustable and you can order different spring rates.||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||02-07-2001||08:51 PM||||batboy, how exactly do coil-overs work? and what are they exactly? as you can see, i don't know anything about suspension stuff. i'm looking to lower my car later in the year and need a good way of doing it. i'm also get different wheels that are wider. and it seems like the will rub on the stock springs.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||jlruiz67||02-08-2001||01:54 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005645.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005645.html</A> ||||reg||14
Z||000012||elfiero||02-08-2001||02:17 PM||||Remember-you can never go too low! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000013||batboy||02-08-2001||06:36 PM||||Well, technically the rear Fiero strut and spring is a type of coil-over shock. What most people refer to as "coil-overs" are adjustable and have easily interchangeable springs. You'll get a fair amount of extra inner tire clearance in the rear. On the front, the coil-overs replace the factory shocks and coil springs. Since I don't have front coil-overs, I don't how much clearance, if any, is gained on the front.||||reg||1
